95	4	83	\N	\N	setting-up-digital-tv	0	2016-07-22 02:22:25.0336	2016-07-29 06:39:48.254927	GeneralContent	\N	published	ecc1f6a1-76ae-4bb6-948a-646cb0065bcd	\N	\N	{"name": "Setting up digital TV", "options": {"toc": 1}, "content_body": "##Overview  \\r\\nAustralian broadcasters transmit more than 20 free-to-air digital TV channels, with support from the Australian Government. All TV channels in Australia are now digital. \\r\\n  \\r\\nThere are 2 ways to get digital TV:  \\r\\n\\r\\n* [with an antenna](#digital-tv-with-an-antenna)  \\r\\n* [with a satellite](#digital-tv-with-a-satellite).  \\r\\n\\r\\n##Digital TV with an antenna  \\r\\n\\r\\nIn most areas, you will need to connect your TV to a suitable antenna to watch free-to-air channels. You can use a VHF antenna or a UHF antenna.  \\r\\n\\r\\nIf your TV is not digital, you will need to connect it to either a digital set-top box or personal video recorder (PVR).  \\r\\n##Digital TV with a satellite  \\r\\n\\r\\nSatellite TV is an option if you have variable or no coverage from the nearest TV transmitter tower.  \\r\\n\\r\\nThe Australian Government funds a free-to-air satellite service called Viewer Access Satellite Television (VAST). It provides channels from national broadcasters (ABC and SBS) and commercial broadcasters (the Seven, Nine and Ten Networks).  \\r\\n\\r\\nAccessing channels is free but you will need to buy your own satellite dish and other equipment.  \\r\\n\\r\\nYou may be eligible for VAST if:  \\r\\n\\r\\n* you live in a remote area with variable or no coverage  \\r\\n* a hill, large tree or other structure blocks your TV signal  \\r\\n* local interference causes problems with your TV signal  \\r\\n* you are travelling in a mobile home or caravan and need temporary access to satellite TV.  \\r\\n  \\r\\n###How to get satellite TV using the VAST service  \\r\\n\\r\\nGo to the [MySwitch tool](/myswitch.digitalready.gov.au) and enter your address. This will tell you how far you are from the nearest TV transmitter and whether you are eligible to apply for VAST. It will also guide you through the application process.  \\r\\n\\r\\nIf you need help with your application, contact the VAST administrator (RBA Holdings Pty Ltd):  \\r\\n\\r\\n**Email:**  \\r\\n[vast.administration@mysattv.com.au](mailto:vast.administration@mysattv.com.au)  \\r\\n\\r\\n**Phone:**  \\r\\n[1300 993 376](tel:1300993376)  \\r\\n\\r\\n###Problems with VAST reception  \\r\\n\\r\\nGo to the [VAST website](https://www.mysattv.com.au/Troubleshooting.aspx) for troubleshooting tips.\\r\\n\\r\\nYou can also request help using their [online form](http://www.mysattv.com.au/Enquiry.aspx).\\r\\n\\r\\n###Complaints about VAST applications\\r\\n\\r\\nYou can lodge a complaint with the Australian Communications and Media Authority (ACMA) if the VAST administrator:  \\r\\n\\r\\n* rejected your application for VAST  \\r\\n* approved your access to VAST but later rejected it  \\r\\n* has not processed your application within 15 business days.  \\r\\n\\r\\nIf you decide to complain, include this information:  \\r\\n\\r\\n* your name  \\r\\n* your contact information (email, telephone and postal address)  \\r\\n* the address VAST services were requested  \\r\\n* the date you applied for VAST  \\r\\n* a copy of your application if available  \\r\\n* the reception equipment you own  \\r\\n* the issue  \\r\\n* any correspondence between you and the VAST administrator.  \\r\\n\\r\\nYou can make your complaint via email, post or fax:\\r\\n\\r\\n**Email:**  \\r\\n[info@acma.gov.au](mailto:info@acma.gov.au)  \\r\\nUse the subject line ‘Satellite TV Complaint’  \\r\\n\\r\\n**Post:**  \\r\\nSatellite TV Complaint  \\r\\nAustralian Communications and Media Authority  \\r\\nPO Box 78  \\r\\nBelconnen ACT 2616    \\r\\n\\r\\n**Fax:**  \\r\\n02 6219 5347  \\r\\n  \\r\\n###Government subsidies  \\r\\n\\r\\nThe Australian Government funds the Viewer Access Satellite Television (VAST) service.  \\r\\n\\r\\nThe Satellite Subsidy Scheme (SSS) and the Household Assistance Scheme (HAS) have now finished.  \\r\\n\\r\\nIf you have questions about an installation under the SSS or the HAS, contact the service provider who did the installation. The phone number should be on the sticker attached to the set-top box or information card you were given at the time of installation.", "short_summary": "You can watch free-to-air digital TV channels with an antenna or a satellite."}	2016-07-29 06:39:48.252183
96	4	83	\N	\N	troubleshooting-tv-reception	1	2016-07-22 02:24:35.588728	2016-07-29 06:43:52.819419	GeneralContent	\N	published	b0cad644-b9b9-46e4-8a7c-ed6473305ab4	\N	\N	{"name": "Troubleshooting TV reception", "options": {"toc": 1}, "content_body": "##Check cables  \\r\\n\\r\\nCheck that your cables are in good condition and properly connected to both the wall socket and TV equipment.  \\r\\n\\r\\n* use shielded coaxial cables and fly leads with F-type connectors\\r\\n* keep TV cables at least 5cm from power points and power cords\\r\\n* keep cables straight and short where possible\\r\\n* avoid connecting multiple cables together\\r\\n* avoid using signal splitters.\\r\\n\\r\\n###Need more help?  \\r\\n\\r\\nIf the other steps on this page don't fix your problem, contact a [professional antenna installer](#). They can check your cables and help you fix related issues.\\r\\n\\r\\n##Retune TV channels  \\r\\n\\r\\nCheck that you’re tuned in to the most up-to-date TV channel frequencies.  \\r\\n\\r\\nTo do this, first auto-tune your TV.  \\r\\n\\r\\nOn most TVs you do this by using your remote control.  \\r\\n\\r\\n* press the MENU button  \\r\\n* look for ‘set-up’ options  \\r\\n* choose ‘auto-tuning’  \\r\\n\\r\\nIf auto-tune does not improve your reception, this may be because you live in an area covered by more than 1 TV transmitter. Your TV may be tuned to weaker signals.  \\r\\n\\r\\nThe next step is to tune it manually.  \\r\\n\\r\\n* use the [MySwitch tool](http://myswitch.digitalready.gov.au) to find the TV transmitter giving the best coverage in your area  \\r\\n* press the MENU button on your TV’s remote control  \\r\\n* look for 'set-up' options  \\r\\n* choose 'manual tune'  \\r\\n* select the channels suggested by the MySwitch tool.  \\r\\n\\r\\n###Need more help?  \\r\\nIf the other steps on this page don't fix your problem, contact a [professional antenna installer](#). They can check your tuning and help fix related issues.\\r\\n\\r\\n##Turn off lights and appliances  \\r\\n\\r\\nElectrical equipment and appliances can cause interference to TV reception. This is a likely cause when poor reception only happens sometimes.\\r\\n\\r\\nTry turning off electrical items—like hair dryers, LED lights or washing machines—and see if this improves your TV reception. \\r\\n\\r\\n###Need more help?\\r\\nIf the other steps on this page don't fix your problem, contact a [professional antenna installer](#). They can check if interference if causing your problems and help you with the next steps.\\r\\n\\r\\n##Check TV antenna  \\r\\n\\r\\nMake sure your TV antenna is in good condition and set up correctly.  \\r\\n\\r\\n* you should have just 1 antenna  \\r\\n* it should be outdoors  \\r\\n* it should not be rusty or broken.  \\r\\n\\r\\nFor the best TV reception, avoid these types of antenna:  \\r\\n\\r\\n* indoor antennas (such as ‘rabbit ears’)  \\r\\n* multiple antennas—these are sometimes installed to receive signals from more than 1 site but can create interference.  \\r\\n* antennas designed to receive FM radio or TV channels in the VHF bands 1 and 2.\\r\\n\\r\\n###Need more help?  \\r\\nIf you think there is a problem with your antenna, contact a [professional antenna installer](#). They will check you have the right antenna and make sure it’s directed to the best TV transmitter for your property. \\r\\n\\r\\n##Ask your neighbours  \\r\\n\\r\\nAsk a few of your neighbours if they have the same problem with their TV reception. Find out which channels are affected.\\r\\n\\r\\n###Contact the broadcaster if:  \\r\\n\\r\\n* you have poor reception on only 1 TV channel (for example, ABC)  \\r\\n* you have poor reception on more than 1 channel provided by only 1 broadcaster (for example, ABC, ABC 2 and ABC 24).  \\r\\n\\r\\nGo to the [SBS](http://www.sbs.com.au/aboutus/contact) and [ABC](http://www.abc.net.au/reception/contact) websites for contact information.\\r\\n\\r\\nCommercial broadcasters are listed on the [FreeTV website](http://www.freetv.com.au/content_common/pg-tv-station-members.seo).\\r\\n\\r\\n###Contact the Australian Communications and Media Authority (ACMA):  \\r\\n\\r\\nIf you and your neighbours have poor TV reception across more than 1 broadcaster, the problem may be caused by external interference such as power lines or street lamps. \\r\\n\\r\\nThe ACMA can investigate and fix interference issues.\\r\\n\\r\\nFirst, contact a [professional antenna installer](#) in your area. They will look at your antenna set up, measure signal levels and help you complete the ACMA’s [online form](#).\\r\\n\\r\\n###Need more information about interference?  \\r\\n\\r\\nContact the ACMA:\\r\\n\\r\\n**Email:**  \\r\\n[info@acma.gov.au](mailto:info@acma.gov.au)  \\r\\n\\r\\n**Phone:**  \\r\\n[1300 850 115](tel:1300850115)  \\r\\n\\r\\n##Check local TV coverage  \\r\\n  \\r\\nCheck the TV transmitter coverage in your local area using the [MySwitch tool](http://myswitch.digitalready.gov.au/).  \\r\\n\\r\\n* If you are in an area with good coverage, try the other troubleshooting tips on this page.  \\r\\n* If you are in an area with variable or no coverage, you may need to [boost your TV signal](television-tv-reception/troubleshooting-tv-reception#boost-tv-signal) or [set up satellite TV](/television-tv-reception/troubleshooting-tv-reception#set-up-satellite-tv).\\r\\n\\r\\n###Temporary issues  \\r\\n\\r\\nSometimes temporary issues, like transmitter maintenance or storm damage, can affect your TV reception. Check there are no [temporary reception issues](/television-tv-reception/areas-with-temporary-reception-issues) in your area.  \\r\\n\\r\\n##Boost TV signal  \\r\\n\\r\\nSignal boosters can improve TV reception if you live in an area surrounded by local obstacles like trees or tall buildings. However, boosters can cause more problems if they are not installed correctly.  \\r\\n\\r\\nThere are 2 main types of signal booster:  \\r\\n\\r\\n* masthead amplifiers  \\r\\n* distribution amplifiers  \\r\\n\\r\\n###When to install a signal booster  \\r\\n\\r\\nCheck the TV transmitter coverage in your area using the [MySwitch tool](http://myswitch.digitalready.gov.au). If you are in an area with variable coverage, a signal booster may help. \\r\\n\\r\\nContact a [professional antenna installer](#) in your area. They can assess if a masthead or distribution amplifier is the best way to improve your TV reception.  \\r\\n \\r\\n##Set up satellite TV  \\r\\nSatellite TV is an option if you have variable or no coverage from the nearest TV transmitter tower. You can check the TV transmitter coverage in your local area using the [MySwitch tool](http://myswitch.digitalready.gov.au). \\r\\n\\r\\nThe Australian Government funds a free-to-air satellite service called Viewer Access Satellite Television (VAST).  \\r\\n\\r\\n[Find out if you're eligible for VAST](/television-tv-reception/setting-up-digital-tv#digital-tv-with-a-satellite)\\r\\n\\r\\n###Fixing satellite TV reception  \\r\\n\\r\\nGo to the [VAST website](http://www.mysattv.com.au/Troubleshooting.aspx) for troubleshooting tips.\\r\\n\\r\\nYou can also request help using their [online form](http://www.mysattv.com.au/Enquiry.aspx).", "short_summary": "Follow these steps to fix free-to-air TV reception and signal issues."}	2016-07-29 06:43:52.816201
