79	11	91	\N	Responsibilities	responsibilities	0	2016-07-14 01:39:24.58956	2016-07-27 04:01:39.940719	GeneralContent	\N	published	7be90974-20b2-4220-a75a-2dc02c727e3f	\N	\N	{"name": "Responsibilities", "content_body": "The Prime Minister is the most powerful person in Parliament. They have many responsibilities including:\\r\\n\\r\\n* chairing meetings in which the government discusses policies (plans for an issue and how to achieve them) and bills (proposed laws)\\r\\n* selecting members of the government to be ministers\\r\\n* leading Cabinet (the main decision-making group of Ministers) in choosing government policies\\r\\n* acting as the chief government spokesperson\\r\\n* representing the Australian Government overseas\\r\\n* advising the Governor-General about constitutional matters and the appointment of ambassadors and heads of government departments\\r\\n* deciding when to call a federal election and leading the government in the election\\r\\n\\r\\nThe Prime Minister also works with the [Department of the Prime Minister and Cabinet](/department-of-the-prime-minister-and-cabinet) and other government agencies within the Prime Minister and Cabinet portfolio.\\r\\n\\r\\nAll ministers are allocated an area of responsibility for how Australia is run. This area of responsibility is known as a portfolio.\\r\\n\\r\\nThe Department and agencies provide information and services related to various portfolio issues such as [national security, gender equality, climate change and more](/department-of-the-prime-minister-and-cabinet/our-role).", "short_summary": "The Prime Minister’s leadership responsibilities include selecting ministers, overseeing the work of the government and acting as its spokesperson in Australia and overseas."}	2016-07-27 04:01:39.938519
