227	6	\N	\N	\N	2016-07-11-norfolk-island	0	2016-07-27 03:40:09.58677	2016-07-27 04:55:56.616979	NewsArticle	{"release_date": "2016-07-11"}	published	05c127bb-bd05-4c91-938c-980c08ff095e	\N	\N	{"name": "Norfolk Island", "options": {}, "summary": "", "content_body": "## Australian Government reform\\r\\n\\r\\nOn 19 March 2015, the Australian Government announced comprehensive reforms for Norfolk Island. We were part of the joint agency taskforce coordinating the Norfolk Island reform process, led by the [Department of Infrastructure and Regional Development](/department-of-infrastructure-and-regional-development).\\r\\n\\r\\nOn 1 July 2016, the Australian Government integrated Norfolk Island with Australia’s tax, health and social security systems and commenced delivering essential national functions such as immigration, customs and biosecurity in Norfolk Island.\\r\\n\\r\\n## Arrangements in Norfolk Island from 1 July 2016\\r\\n\\r\\nWe are now delivering immigration and customs functions in Norfolk Island, where we have a permanent Australian Border Force (ABF) presence.\\r\\n\\r\\nFor immigration purposes, Norfolk Island has been integrated into the Australian migration zone. This means that passenger movements between the Australian mainland and Norfolk Island are considered domestic travel. All people (non-citizens as well as Australian citizens) travelling between Norfolk Island and an overseas destination are subject to Australian immigration requirements in accordance with the Australian *Migration Act 1958* and *Migration Regulations 1994*. For non-citizens, this includes being required to hold an appropriate Australian visa to enter, visit or reside in Norfolk Island. With the introduction of these new immigration arrangements, the Norfolk Island *Immigration Act 1980* was repealed.\\r\\n\\r\\nFor customs purposes, people and goods arriving in and leaving from Norfolk Island will remain international movements. The Norfolk Island Customs Ordinance 2016 applies a modified version of the *Customs Act 1901*, the *Customs Regulation 2015* and the *Customs (International Obligations) Regulation 2015* in Norfolk Island. The *Customs (Prohibited Imports) Regulations 1956* and the *Customs (Prohibited Exports) Regulations 1958* also apply. With the introduction of these new customs arrangements, the Norfolk Island *Customs Act 1913* and *Customs Regulations 1986* were repealed.  \\r\\n\\r\\nThere are no customs duties, GST or other indirect taxes (for example, Luxury Car Tax) on goods arriving in Norfolk Island.\\r\\n\\r\\nThe current mainland Australia requirements for goods exported to and imported from Norfolk Island have not changed (for example, export and import declarations are still required). Implementation of customs functions in Norfolk Island is consistent with existing arrangements in the Indian Ocean Territories of Christmas Island and Cocos (Keeling) Islands.", "short_summary": "Arrangements in Norfolk Island from 1 July 2016"}	2016-07-27 04:55:56.614487
102	4	83	\N	\N	areas-with-temporary-reception-issues	2	2016-07-22 02:51:25.554887	2016-07-29 06:44:28.306108	GeneralContent	\N	published	c55862f4-f91d-4afc-83fe-3d5932c0e5dc	\N	\N	{"name": "Areas with temporary reception issues", "options": {"toc": 0}, "content_body": "##New South Wales  \\r\\n\\r\\n###[Hunter region](#)  \\r\\nIncluding: Port Stephens area from Tea Gardens to Salt Ash, Anna Bay, Tanilba Bay, Karuah, Swan Bay, Medowie, Bulahdelah  \\r\\n\\r\\n###[Yass](#)  \\r\\n\\r\\n###[Central Western Slopes](#)  \\r\\nIncluding: Gulgong, Narromine, Trangie and possibly Warren  \\r\\n\\r\\n###[Shoalhaven](#)    \\r\\nNowra, Kiama and surrounding Shoalhaven area  \\r\\n\\r\\n##Victoria  \\r\\n  \\r\\n###[Angelsea](#)  \\r\\nIncluding: Anglesea, Aireys Inlet, Fairhaven  \\r\\n\\r\\n###[East Gippsland](#)  \\r\\nIncluding: Lakes Entrance, Bairnsdale, Orbost, Eagle Point, Metung, Newlands Arm, Nicholson, Marlo, Paynesville, Raymond Island, Swan Reach  \\r\\n\\r\\n###[Apollo Bay](#)  \\r\\nIncluding: Apollo Bay and Skenes Creek  \\r\\n\\r\\n##Western Australia  \\r\\n\\r\\n###[Dongara/Port Denison](#)  ", "short_summary": "These locations have TV reception problems caused by temporary issues like interference, planned maintenance or storm damage."}	2016-07-29 06:44:28.303768
284	13	\N	\N	\N	2016-07-18-agreement-boosts-trans-tasman-trade	0	2016-08-01 02:13:48.173044	2016-08-01 02:13:50.379381	NewsArticle	{"release_date": "2016-07-18"}	published	c44c984f-8992-4782-bea1-be0088a0c3cb	\N	\N	{"name": "Agreement boosts trans-Tasman trade", "options": {}, "content_body": "The [Department of Immigration and Border Protection](/department-of-immigration-and-border-protection) and the New Zealand Customs Service signed a Mutual Recognition Agreement (MRA) in Brussels last week to recognise the supply chain security programmes of both countries.\\r\\n\\r\\nThe [Minister for Immigration and Border Protection](/minister-for-immigration-and-border-protection) Peter Dutton said that this was a significant step forward in Australia's relationship with its seventh largest trading partner.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\"This is another great step forward in our trading relationship with New Zealand as the agreement would reduce the regulatory burden for exporters and make Trans-Tasman trade more appealing for businesses,\\" Mr Dutton said.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\"By recognising the supply chain security measures Australian and New Zealand businesses already have in place in their home country, we can facilitate the movement of trade faster.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\"About $3 billion New Zealand imports to Australia will be fast tracked and $7.5 billion of Australian exports to New Zealand will benefit from the MRA by 2020.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\"It means that it will take less time for exporters with approved measures in place to get their goods to market than our international competitors.\\"\\r\\n\\r\\n##Contact \\r\\nMinister's Office  \\r\\n[02 6277 7860](tel:0262777860)", "short_summary": "A new agreement between Australia and New Zealand will drive the international competitiveness of export businesses in both countries."}	2016-08-01 02:13:50.376936
87	8	81	\N	\N	department-of-finance	5	2016-07-14 04:41:27.047185	2016-08-01 03:39:23.711404	SectionHome	\N	published	5b682ea5-61b1-42da-97c8-a5adb81572bf	\N	\N	{"name": "Department of Finance", "content_body": "## Information & services\\r\\n\\r\\nThe Budget, financial reporting processes, asset management and more.\\r\\n\\r\\n[items pulled in automatically (Budget, government advertising, Parliamentary entitlements)]\\r\\n\\r\\n[All information and services](/department-of-finance/our-role#information-and-services)\\r\\n\\r\\n## Our ministers\\r\\n\\r\\nOur Department assists the Ministers in the Australian Government’s Finance portfolio.\\r\\n\\r\\n[Ministers pulled in automatically]", "short_summary": "We help the Australian Government to manage its finances by providing advice and services."}	2016-08-01 03:39:23.708149
78	11	91	\N	Contact	contact	3	2016-07-14 01:39:11.704688	2016-07-29 06:24:24.452377	GeneralContent	\N	published	a828a4d7-abdb-41f2-9008-dc30776eb798	\N	\N	{"name": "Contact", "content_body": "## Parliament Office\\r\\n\\r\\n### Email\\r\\n\\r\\n[__________@__________.gov.au](mailto:__________@__________.gov.au)\\r\\n\\r\\n[Contact form](https://www.pm.gov.au/contact-your-pm)\\r\\n\\r\\n### Phone\\r\\n\\r\\n[02 6277 7700](tel:0262777700)  \\r\\n9am to 5pm AEST (Australian Eastern Standard Time)  \\r\\nMonday to Friday excluding ACT public holidays\\r\\n\\r\\nFax:  02 6273 4100\\r\\n\\r\\n### Postal address\\r\\n\\r\\nPrime Minister  \\r\\nParliament House  \\r\\nCANBERRA ACT 2600\\r\\n\\r\\n### Social media\\r\\n\\r\\nMr Turnbull’s personal accounts.\\r\\n\\r\\nFacebook: [Malcolm Turnbull](https://www.facebook.com/malcolmturnbull)  \\r\\nTwitter: [@TurnbullMalcolm](https://twitter.com/TurnbullMalcolm)  \\r\\nYoutube: [Malcolm Turnbull](https://www.youtube.com/user/MalcolmTurnbullMP)  \\r\\nInstagram: [Malcolm Turnbull](https://www.instagram.com/turnbullmalcolm/)\\r\\n\\r\\n### Privacy\\r\\n\\r\\nAny personal details you share with us will only be used to reply to your message and not for any other purpose, except as authorised by the [Privacy Act](#).", "short_summary": "You can contact the Prime Minister in a number of ways."}	2016-07-29 06:24:24.450139
84	5	81	\N	\N	department-of-health	2	2016-07-14 04:41:26.962767	2016-08-01 03:39:48.464074	SectionHome	\N	published	8dd1d729-00f3-4934-b3f3-ba334fd6e09f	\N	\N	{"name": "Department of Health", "content_body": "## Information & services\\r\\n\\r\\nHealth conditions, solutions, insurance and more.\\r\\n\\r\\n[items pulled in automatically (Ageing and aged care, Health emergencies, My Health Record)]\\r\\n\\r\\n[All information and services](/department-of-health/our-role#information-and-services)\\r\\n\\r\\n## Our ministers\\r\\n\\r\\nOur Department assists the Ministers in the Australian Government’s Health and Aged Care portfolio.\\r\\n\\r\\n[Ministers pulled in automatically]", "short_summary": "We lead and shape Australia’s health and sport systems through policy, programs and regulation."}	2016-08-01 03:39:48.46065
71	12	92	\N	Responsibilities	responsibilities	0	2016-07-14 01:31:54.220469	2016-07-29 03:14:00.559927	GeneralContent	\N	published	0b68479b-15b9-493b-9f6e-063331c933d3	\N	\N	{"name": "Responsibilities", "content_body": "The Minister leads the [Department of Health](/department-of-health) and other government agencies within the health and aged care portfolio.\\r\\n\\r\\nMinisters are members of the Australian Government who have been allocated an area of responsibility for how Australia is run. This area of responsibility is known as a portfolio.\\r\\n\\r\\nThe Department and agencies provide information and services related to various portfolio responsibilities such as [health services, diseases, conditions and more](/department-of-health/our-role).\\r\\n\\r\\nThe minister works with their department, agencies, community organisations and professional associations to:\\r\\n\\r\\n* Develop policies related to health and aged care issues. A policy states the Government’s plans for an issue and how to achieve them.\\r\\n* Prepare new laws.\\r\\n* Change existing laws which need updating or improving.\\r\\n* Introduce bills (suggestions for new or changed laws) into the Parliament and explain why they are necessary and how they will solve health and aged care problems. If a bill becomes a law, the minister and their department are responsible for putting that law into action.", "short_summary": "The Minister for Health and Aged Care develops policies and laws to improve Australia’s health and aged care services."}	2016-07-29 03:14:00.557628
219	2	\N	\N	\N	2016-04-29-nbn-satellite-service-opens-for-business	0	2016-07-26 23:12:17.016256	2016-07-27 04:57:15.566385	NewsArticle	{"release_date": "2016-04-29"}	published	c1177ff2-cda2-4d48-b284-bda76634a4a3	\N	\N	{"name": "nbn satellite service opens for business", "options": {}, "summary": "", "content_body": "nbn has commenced migrating users from the interim satellite service and will start connecting new eligible customers across the country - many of whom have previously had no access to broadband services at all. The rollout will take time however, as the new technology requires nbn to visit each home and business to install the equipment to connect to the satellite.\\r\\n\\r\\nWith download speeds of up to 25 Megabits per second (Mbps) and 5 Mbps uploads, the satellite service is one of the best ever launched in the world.\\r\\n\\r\\nTony Cross, Chief Network Architect at nbn, spoke to us about what the satellites will offer. \\r\\n\\r\\n“There’s no doubt that this will be a world class service... It’s completely different from anything that anybody would have seen before with satellite in Australia,” he said.\\r\\n\\r\\nThe nbn satellite service has been years in the making and the satellites have been specifically designed to provide fast broadband from space.\\r\\n\\r\\n“The type of satellite that we’ve designed and built effectively operates as a relay. We send signals up to it, it receives then, amplifies them and then sends the signals back down. We have nine earth stations to connect up to the satellite, with a tenth one for the purposes where we might need to maintain one of the other nine” he said.\\r\\n\\r\\nnbn will launch a second satellite later this year.\\r\\n\\r\\n[See nbn’s website for information on eligibility](http://www.nbnco.com.au/connect-home-or-business/information-for-home/satellite.html)  ", "short_summary": "Fast broadband is now available those living in regional Australia with the nbn satellite Sky Muster offering commercial services from today."}	2016-07-27 04:57:15.564112
270	11	\N	\N	\N	2016-07-28-cabinet-remarks	0	2016-07-28 23:52:15.087454	2016-08-01 04:25:42.26629	NewsArticle	{"release_date": "2016-07-28"}	published	6984f2b4-806e-4c6e-8223-0593f3e11ef1	\N	\N	{"name": "Cabinet remarks", "options": {}, "content_body": "**PRIME MINISTER:** Well, colleagues, welcome. Three years of Government, this is the term of delivery.\\r\\n\\r\\nWe are returned to Government, majority Government, with a big program - a program based on our national economic plan that we took to the election, a plan designed to set Australia up for the future.\\r\\n\\r\\nWe have so much to do, so many projects, so many programs to deliver.\\r\\n\\r\\nWe have a big task of Budget repair. We've set out the way we'll do that in the Budget.\\r\\n\\r\\nWe've got big infrastructure projects that we're committed to, completing the NBN in this term. We have got big projects of new infrastructure - Western Sydney airport, inland rail, right across the country - we are determined to deliver Australians the prosperity, the economic growth and the jobs that they deserve.\\r\\n\\r\\nSo there is lots to do. We have plenty of work ahead of us, we've got a big task and a great trust that's been reposed in us by the Australian people. We're equal to it and we're down to work.\\r\\n\\r\\nThank you all and congratulations. We are returned, we're back in Government and we're back serving the Australian people, securing their future.", "short_summary": "Speech, Canberra, Errors and omissions excepted"}	2016-07-28 23:52:18.82298
65	10	90	\N	Contact	contact	3	2016-07-14 01:30:00.900622	2016-07-27 05:45:10.894795	GeneralContent	\N	published	7b1e8c0f-b468-4d93-bff7-a8e775f2a623	\N	\N	{"name": "Contact", "content_body": "## Parliament Office\\r\\n\\r\\n### Email\\r\\n\\r\\n[financeminister@finance.gov.au](mailto:financeminister@finance.gov.au)\\r\\n\\r\\n[Contact form](#)\\r\\n\\r\\n### Phone\\r\\n\\r\\n[02 6215 2222](tel:0262152222)  \\r\\n9am to 5pm AEST (Australian Eastern Standard Time)  \\r\\nMonday to Friday excluding ACT public holidays\\r\\n\\r\\n### Postal address\\r\\n\\r\\nMinister for Finance  \\r\\nPO Box 6100  \\r\\nSenate  \\r\\nParliament House  \\r\\nCanberra ACT 2600\\r\\n  \\r\\n### Social media\\r\\n\\r\\nSenator Cormann's personal accounts.  \\r\\n\\r\\nFacebook: [Mathias Cormann](https://www.facebook.com/SenatorMathiasCormann/)  \\r\\nTwitter: [@MathiasCormann](https://twitter.com/mathiascormann)\\r\\n\\r\\n### Privacy\\r\\n\\r\\nAny personal details you share with us will only be used to reply to your message and not for any other purpose, except as authorised by the [Privacy Act](#).", "short_summary": "You can contact the Minister in a number of ways."}	2016-07-27 05:45:10.892564
229	2	\N	\N	\N	2016-05-06-aged-under-15-what-does-it-mean-for-classifications	0	2016-07-27 04:00:34.041558	2016-07-27 04:25:07.538657	NewsArticle	{"release_date": "2016-05-06"}	published	695b372b-0d14-41d1-a714-b87f3430c7ba	\N	\N	{"name": "Aged under 15: what does it mean for classifications?", "options": {}, "summary": "", "content_body": "So how are they different?\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\n![PG: Parental guidance recommended](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/AusDTO/gov-au-images/master/PG.jpg)\\r\\n\\r\\n**PG content is not recommended for viewing by anyone under 15 without guidance from a parent or guardian**. Some younger children may also find PG films and computer games confusing or upsetting, so it’s good for them to have a trusted adult around to explain what is going on.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\n![M: Recommended for mature audiences](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/AusDTO/gov-au-images/master/M.jpg)\\r\\n\\r\\nContent classified **M is not recommended for anyone under 15 years of age**. Although not legally restricted, it’s definitely higher level content than PG, so these computer games and films are generally better for older kids.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\r\\n![MA 15+ Restricted: Not suitable for people under 15. Under 15s must be accompaned by a parent or adult guardan](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/AusDTO/gov-au-images/master/MA.jpg)\\r\\n\\r\\nSee the red stop sign? The “15+” and “restricted” are important. **MA 15+ content is legally restricted to persons 15 years and over unless they are accompanied by a parent or guardian**. These films and games are **unsuitable for people under 15**. A parent or guardian may choose to take under 15’s along to see an MA 15+ film, but it could contain material such as strong sex scenes or drug use, and social issues that require a mature perspective.\\r\\n\\r\\nUnder 15’s playing MA 15+ games or viewing MA 15+ films must be accompanied the whole time. Cinemas cannot allow anyone under 15 into the theatre without a parent or guardian – if you decide to let them see an MA 15+ film you need you to be with them, not out shopping! The same goes for home entertainment - content doesn’t change when computer games and films are viewed at home, and kids aged under 15 still need you to be there for them around such strong material.\\r\\n\\r\\nClassifications are your resource to make informed decisions about what you and your children see and play.\\r\\n\\r\\nBefore buying that game or selecting a movie, check the classification of a title on the National Classification Database at [www.classification.gov.au](http://www.classification.gov.au).", "short_summary": "Did you know that three classification categories have something in common? The PG, M and MA 15+ classifications all refer to 15 years of age in terms of their suitability."}	2016-07-27 04:25:07.536268
33	6	85	\N	Our responsibilities	our-role	0	2016-07-06 00:57:39.912652	2016-07-28 21:14:16.427337	GeneralContent	\N	published	6f47b1bf-8153-4dba-9b21-8023ed5ec694	\N	\N	{"name": "Our role", "options": {"toc": 1, "suppress_in_nav": false}, "content_body": "## What we do\\r\\n\\r\\nOur mission is to protect Australia’s border and manage the ​movement of people and goods across it.\\r\\n\\r\\nOur work supports the Australian Government to achieve strong national security, a healthy economy and a cohesive society.\\r\\n\\r\\nOur responsibilities include:\\r\\n\\r\\n* Migration programs\\r\\n* Humanitarian programs\\r\\n* Australian citizenship\\r\\n* Trade and customs\\r\\n* Offshore maritime security\\r\\n* Revenue collection\\r\\n\\r\\nWithin our Department:\\r\\n\\r\\n* The **[Australian Border Force](#)** agency is responsible for our investigations and immigration detention operations.\\r\\n* **[The Office of the Migration Agents Registration Authority (OMARA)](#)** regulates the Australian migration agent industry.\\r\\n\\r\\n[Read more about what we do](#)\\r\\n\\r\\n## Information and services\\r\\n\\r\\nWe provide information and deliver services related to immigration and border protection.\\r\\n\\r\\n**[Citizenship](#)**  \\r\\nInformation on Australian citizenship including applications, dual citizenship and how to obtain proof of citizenship.\\r\\n\\r\\n**[Visas](#)**  \\r\\nInformation on applying for and renewing visas including health requirements, ImmiAccount and fees.\\r\\n\\r\\n**[Visiting Australia](#)**  \\r\\nInformation on coming to Australia for a holiday, a business trip or to visit family.\\r\\n\\r\\n**[Working in Australia](#)**  \\r\\nInformation on coming to Australia to work including legal requirements, skilled occupations and regional employment.\\r\\n\\r\\n**[Studying in Australia](#)**  \\r\\nInformation for students who are considering education courses in Australia.\\r\\n\\r\\n**[Moving to Australia](#)**  \\r\\nInformation on migrating to Australia including legal requirements and tips and advice for living here.\\r\\n\\r\\n**[Humanitarian issues](#)**  \\r\\nInformation on Australia's refugee and humanitarian program and ImmiCard.", "short_summary": "We manage programs and services related to migration, citizenship, trade, customs, offshore maritime security and revenue collection."}	2016-07-28 21:14:16.424553
272	8	\N	\N	\N	2016-05-10-woag-accommodation-arrangement	0	2016-07-29 00:36:12.500973	2016-07-29 00:36:15.291393	NewsArticle	{"release_date": "2016-05-10"}	published	2ea74108-d8fe-44ea-9828-14472d476bf8	\N	\N	{"name": "WoAG Accommodation Arrangement", "options": {}, "content_body": "Finance is seeking comments on a discussion paper that may inform the decision on what form a future accommodation program for the Government may take.\\r\\n\\r\\nConsultation is open until 5 pm on 27 May 2016. You can provide comments directly on this blog, or if you would prefer, via email to [hoteltender@finance.gov.au](mailto:hoteltender@finance.gov.au).\\r\\n\\r\\nAttached for your review and comment are the following documents:\\r\\n\\r\\n* [Accommodation Discussion Paper May 2016.pdf (228 KB)](http://finance.gov.au/sites/default/files/Accommodation%20Discussion%20Paper%20May%202016_0.pdf)\\r\\n* [Accommodation Discussion Paper May 2016.docx (45 KB)](http://finance.gov.au/sites/default/files/Accommodation%20Discussion%20Paper%20May%202016_0.docx)\\r\\n\\r\\nI look forward to seeing some innovative ideas.", "short_summary": "The current Whole of Australian Government’s accommodation program management arrangement is due to expire in June 2017."}	2016-07-29 00:36:15.28926
271	2	\N	\N	\N	2016-07-27-support-for-confluence-festival-of-india-in-australia-2016	0	2016-07-29 00:00:38.779465	2016-08-01 01:25:33.430784	NewsArticle	{"release_date": "2016-07-27"}	published	888392d9-ac33-4d15-848e-1848b4b50a9b	\N	\N	{"name": "Support for Confluence Festival of India in Australia 2016", "options": {}, "content_body": "![Confluence: Festival of India in Australia 2016](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/AusDTO/gov-au-images/master/confluence-festival-of-india-in-australia_1.jpg)\\r\\n\\r\\nThe festival is the most significant showcase of India's arts and culture to be staged in Australia. Held from August to November 2016, the festival will bring an exciting and diverse program of the finest classical and contemporary performing and visual arts from India to audiences across six Australian cities. The festival will also include a number of community activities and collaborations with local artists.\\r\\n\\r\\nMedia release: [Support for Festival of India in Australia 2016, 26 July 2016](/minister-for-communications/news/2016-07-26-support-for-festival-of-india-in-australia-2016)", "short_summary": "Confluence: Festival of India in Australia 2016 has received Australian Government funding of $250,000."}	2016-08-01 01:25:33.428519
60	9	89	\N	Contact	contact	3	2016-07-14 01:01:57.890913	2016-08-01 02:15:44.478212	GeneralContent	\N	published	f6611917-0312-4308-8a54-d4918a3ce0c8	\N	\N	{"name": "Contact", "content_body": "## Parliament Office\\r\\n\\r\\n### Email\\r\\n\\r\\n[minister@communications.gov.au](mailto:minister@communications.gov.au)\\r\\n\\r\\n[Contact form](#)\\r\\n\\r\\n### Phone\\r\\n\\r\\n[02 6277 7480](tel:0262777480)  \\r\\n9am to 5pm AEST (Australian Eastern Standard Time)  \\r\\nMonday to Friday excluding ACT public holidays\\r\\n\\r\\n### Postal address\\r\\n\\r\\nMinister for Communications  \\r\\nPO Box 6100  \\r\\nSenate  \\r\\nParliament House  \\r\\nCanberra ACT 2600\\r\\n\\r\\n### Social media\\r\\n\\r\\nSenator Fifield's personal accounts.\\r\\n\\r\\nFacebook: [Senator Fifield](https://www.facebook.com/SenatorFifield)  \\r\\nTwitter: [@SenatorFifield](https://twitter.com/SenatorFifield)  \\r\\nYoutube: [Senator Fifield](http://www.youtube.com/user/SenatorFifield)  \\r\\n\\r\\n### Privacy\\r\\n\\r\\nAny personal details you share with us will only be used to reply to your message and not for any other purpose, except as authorised by the [Privacy Act](#).", "short_summary": "You can contact the Minister in a number of ways."}	2016-08-01 02:15:44.475947
68	12	92	\N	About the Minister	about-the-minister	1	2016-07-14 01:31:11.263262	2016-07-29 03:01:25.440828	GeneralContent	\N	published	ef0d6ea7-1016-4a7f-a677-704cfc309e5b	\N	\N	{"name": "About the Minister", "content_body": "![The Hon Sussan Ley MP](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/AusDTO/gov-au-images/master/ministers/sussan-ley-fb.jpg)\\r\\n\\r\\n## The Hon Sussan Ley MP\\r\\n\\r\\nLiberal Party of Australia\\r\\n\\r\\n### Political career\\r\\n\\r\\nMs Ley was appointed Minister for Health and Minister for Sport in December 2014. The Aged Care portfolio was added to Ms Ley’s responsibilities on 30 September 2015.\\r\\n\\r\\nShe previously held shadow portfolios for Early Childhood Learning & Employment Participation (2010-2013), Justice and Customs/Assistant Treasurer (2008-2009) and Housing and Women (2007).\\r\\n\\r\\nIn 2006, Ms Ley was the Parliamentary Secretary for Agriculture, Fisheries & Forestry. In 2004, she was the Parliamentary Secretary for Children and Youth Affairs.\\r\\n\\r\\nSussan Ley was first elected as the Federal Member for Farrer in NSW in 2001.\\r\\n\\r\\n[Read more on her personal website](http://sussanley.com/home/)\\r\\n\\r\\n### Life and career outside politics\\r\\n\\r\\nBefore entering Parliament, Ms Ley worked a variety of jobs which provided a range of ‘real life’ experiences. \\r\\n\\r\\nAt age 19, she enrolled in flight school and gained her commercial pilot's licence, which she still holds. This led to roles as an air traffic controller and commercial pilot. \\r\\n\\r\\nMs Ley has also been a shearer’s cook, wool and beef farmer.\\r\\n\\r\\nWhile raising three children, ten years of part time study led to a Bachelor of Economics, Master of Taxation Law and a Master of Accounting before she took up a senior position with the Australian Taxation Office.", "short_summary": "The current Minister for Health and Aged Care is The Hon Sussan Ley MP, Federal Member for Farrer in NSW."}	2016-07-29 03:01:25.438568
50	8	87	\N	Contact us	contact-us	8	2016-07-06 01:03:37.022413	2016-08-02 00:13:45.177534	GeneralContent	\N	published	bf40a3c6-593b-46ec-a1af-d7c5e897f34f	\N	\N	{"name": "Contact us", "options": {"toc": 1}, "content_body": "## Email  \\r\\n\\r\\n**General enquiries**  \\r\\n__________@finance.gov.au\\r\\n\\r\\n**Media enquiries**  \\r\\n[mediaenquiries@finance.gov.au](mailto:mediaenquiries@finance.gov.au)  \\r\\n\\r\\n**Contacting staff members**  \\r\\nTo send an email to officers in the department, use the format:  firstname.lastname@finance.gov.au  \\r\\nYou can also find staff details through the [Government Online Directory](http://www.directory.gov.au).\\r\\n\\r\\n**[Contact form](#)**\\r\\n\\r\\n## Phone  \\r\\n\\r\\n**General enquiries**  \\r\\n[61 2 6215 2222](tel:61262152222)    \\r\\n9am – 5pm AEST (Australian Eastern Standard Time)  \\r\\nMonday to Friday excluding ACT public holidays  \\r\\n\\r\\nIf you have a hearing or speech impairment, use the [National Relay Service](http://relayservice.gov.au/) to access any of our listed phone numbers.  \\r\\nTelephone: [1300 555 727](tel:1300555727)\\r\\n\\r\\n## Locations and opening hours\\r\\n\\r\\n### Canberra\\r\\n\\r\\n9am – 5pm AEST (Australian Eastern Standard Time)  \\r\\nMonday to Friday excluding ACT public holidays  \\r\\n\\r\\nJohn Gorton Building  \\r\\nKing Edward Terrace  \\r\\nParkes ACT 2600  \\r\\n\\r\\nOne Canberra Avenue  \\r\\nForrest ACT 2603  \\r\\n\\r\\nTreasury Building  \\r\\n8 Parkes Place West  \\r\\nParkes ACT 2600  \\r\\n\\r\\nCOMCAR  \\r\\nBuildings 6 & 7, Dairy Road  \\r\\nFyshwick ACT 2609  \\r\\n\\r\\n### Ministerial and Parliamentary Services - State Offices\\r\\n\\r\\n9am – 5pm AEST (Australian Eastern Standard Time)  \\r\\nMonday to Friday excluding ACT public holidays  \\r\\n\\r\\n[New South Wales State Office](#)  \\r\\n[Victorian State Office](#)  \\r\\n[Queensland State Office](#)  \\r\\n[South Australian State Office](#)  \\r\\n[Western Australian State Office](#)  \\r\\n[Tasmanian State Office](#)  \\r\\n[Northern Territory State Office](#)\\r\\n\\r\\n## Postal address\\r\\n\\r\\nDepartment of Finance  \\r\\nJohn Gorton Building  \\r\\nKing Edward Terrace  \\r\\nParkes ACT 2600  \\r\\nAustralia\\r\\n\\r\\n## Social media\\r\\n\\r\\n**Twitter:**  \\r\\n\\r\\n* [@ausgovfinance](https://twitter.com/ausgovfinance)  \\r\\n\\r\\n**LinkedIn:**  \\r\\n\\r\\n* [Australian Government Department of Finance](https://www.linkedin.com/company/department-of-finance-and-deregulation)  \\r\\n\\r\\n**YouTube:**  \\r\\n\\r\\n* [Department Of Finance Australia](https://www.youtube.com/user/DepartmentOfFinance)  \\r\\n\\r\\n## How we handle enquiries\\r\\n\\r\\n**[Client Service Charter](javascript:void(0))**  \\r\\nWe aim to provide you with a high level of service.  \\r\\n\\r\\n**[Complaints Handling Policy](javascript:void(0))**  \\r\\nHow to complain about us and have your concerns addressed.\\r\\n    \\r\\n**[Privacy Policy](javascript:void(0))**  \\r\\nHow we handle personal and sensitive information.  \\r\\n\\r\\n**[Reporting Fraud Policy](javascript:void(0))**  \\r\\nYou are strongly encouraged to report any suspected fraud incidents against us.  \\r\\n    \\r\\n**[Public Interest Disclosure](javascript:void(0))**  \\r\\nHow to report serious wrongdoing in the Commonwealth public sector.  \\r\\n\\r\\n**[Freedom of information (FOI) requests](javascript:void(0))**  \\r\\nLearn more about our FOI obligations and how to make a request.  ", "short_summary": "You can contact us in a number of ways."}	2016-08-02 00:13:45.174666
273	5	\N	\N	\N	2016-03-11-health-secretary-outlines-the-challenges-of-health-reform-to-committee-for-economic-development-of-australia	0	2016-07-29 00:41:49.006987	2016-07-29 00:41:52.053825	NewsArticle	{"release_date": "2016-03-11"}	published	1db5a0a3-dc46-4687-bc90-e6b70853ce15	\N	\N	{"name": "Health Secretary outlines the challenges of health reform to Committee for Economic Development of Australia", "options": {}, "content_body": "The broad reform agenda that the Australian Government is embarking on in health is being enthusiastically embraced by the Federal Department of Health as it moves to better deliver strategic policy advice and action, the Secretary of the Department, Martin Bowles, told health sector leaders at a conference in Brisbane today.\\r\\n\\r\\nSpeaking at a meeting of the Committee for Economic Development of Australia (CEDA), Mr Bowles said modernising Australia's health system to meet the needs and expectations of consumers is a big challenge for the Health bureaucracy but he was confident great changes can be achieved by innovative, data-driven, evidence based policy making.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\"In transforming Australia's health and ageing system we are going through an exciting time of change, of innovation and opportunity, but the pressures are ever present and if not managed properly could overwhelm us,\\" Mr Bowles said.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\"It is for this reason that I have made widespread structural changes within the Department of Health so our people can participate effectively in this unique opportunity for change.\\"\\r\\n\\r\\nMr Bowles said managing an ageing population, an epidemic of chronic disease, outmoded primary care financing arrangements, a disconnected health system and the need to embrace digital technology put an enormous pressure on health resources.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\"This is why the Australian Government has a string of parallel and complementary health reforms currently underway and implementing new strategies from these reviews requires new ways of thinking, new ways of doing things and we need to do things better and innovate. \\r\\n\\r\\n\\"Health Department officers are supporting these expert reviews which are looking at a range of health programs which affect the daily lives of all Australians,\\" Mr Bowles said.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\"These include a review of our Medical Benefits Schedule which contains items and procedures listed on the MBS, some that are long past their use by date with others simply illogical.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\"In addition we have been managing a review into the way primary health care is provided in Australia. This is a landmark review and could result in a whole new way of funding and delivering primary health care, particularly for people with chronic and complex conditions and those living with mental health issues.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\"We are having a national discussion on Private Health Insurance, an issue that the general public has engaged with in their thousands responding to an on-line survey.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\"We are also serious about electronic health records which is the way forward to ensure better health care for all Australians that will no doubt save lives and eliminate a lot of waste in the health system.\\"\\r\\n\\r\\nMr Bowles said the return of aged care to the Health Department will assist in the big challenge now that the Australian population is living so much longer and older. People need the best health care options available to them to live long and happy lives.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\"Aged care is back where I believe it belongs and we have some excellent reforms coming on line to improve the health and wellbeing of older Australians,\\" he said.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\"However, one of my most pressing concerns is that we take advantage of better data collection and reporting to give us the evidence of what is required, what is working and what isn't,\\" Mr Bowles said.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\"We need to put data, analytics, evaluation and research at the centre of health care to make the system work smarter and more effectively.\\"\\r\\n\\r\\nMr Bowles said he saw the Federal Department of Health as a prime leader in the health reform agenda and to this end he has put stronger emphasis on the department's relationships with stakeholders, maximising linkages and building effective, ongoing partnerships including with colleagues in the states and territories.\\r\\n\\r\\n\\"We need to have the courage to try new and innovative ways of doing things and I am confident that every other stakeholder in the health arena sees the same opportunities for reform and are keen to play their part,\\" Mr Bowles said.\\r\\n\\r\\n## Media contact: \\r\\nKay McNiece  \\r\\nPhone: [0412 132 585](tel:0412132585)", "short_summary": "The broad reform agenda that the Australian Government is embarking on in health is being enthusiastically embraced by the Federal Department of Health as it moves to better deliver strategic policy advice and action."}	2016-07-29 00:41:52.051228
274	6	\N	\N	\N	2016-07-29-border-clearance-advice-for-religious-pilgrims	0	2016-07-29 00:48:48.939976	2016-07-29 00:48:52.396013	NewsArticle	{"release_date": "2016-07-29"}	published	f32aa926-a65d-4d71-aa82-8e560b8f27e5	\N	\N	{"name": "Border clearance advice for religious pilgrims", "options": {}, "content_body": "Making the pilgrimage to Mecca is a joyous opportunity for travelling Muslim Australians. We encourage anyone travelling to Saudi Arabia to perform Hajj to familiarise themselves with Australian border processes prior to travel.\\r\\n\\r\\n##Arriving at the airport\\r\\nAll travellers should arrive no later than three hours prior to departure to allow enough time for check-in, border processing and any unexpected delays. After checking-in for your flight you should progress directly to border processing.\\r\\n\\r\\n##Border clearance\\r\\nWe are committed to maintaining the security of our nation and screen all arriving and departing travellers.\\r\\n\\r\\nWhen you are departing, have the following ready for clearance at the border:\\r\\n\\r\\n* passport\\r\\n* completed Outgoing Passenger Card\\r\\n* boarding pass.\\r\\n\\r\\nBefore you arrive into or depart from Australia, Australian Border Force (ABF) officers might examine you and/or the items you are carrying. Officers may inspect all baggage, goods and documents that you are travelling with at the time you arrive or depart Australia.\\r\\n\\r\\nThis includes electronic devices and their contents, such as mobile phones, tablets and laptops, and other storage devices.\\r\\n\\r\\nABF officers assess travellers based on border risk and will never stop individuals based simply on race, religion, ethnicity or travel destination. In all cases, ABF officers are expected to treat travellers with dignity and respect.\\r\\n\\r\\n##Declaring cash or foreign currency\\r\\nThere is no limit to the amount of cash or foreign currency you can bring into or out of Australia. However, if the total value is AUD$10,000 (or the foreign currency equivalent) or more, you must declare it at the Australian border.\\r\\n\\r\\nThis includes Australian dollars, foreign currency, or any combination of this, being carried on your person, in your baggage or if someone else is carrying it for you.\\r\\n\\r\\nTo declare you must:\\r\\n\\r\\n* answer 'Yes' to the relevant questions on your Outgoing Passenger Card\\r\\n* complete a Cross-Border Movement - Physical Currency (CBM-PC) reporting form, available from ABF officers at international airports.\\r\\n\\r\\nSharing money between people travelling with you to avoid reporting requirements is called 'structuring' and is against the law.\\r\\n\\r\\n##Know what is on your electronic devices\\r\\nIt is an offence to bring into or take out of Australia, material that is offensive, or advocating violence in any form. This includes images or videos held on electronic devices that portray cruel and revolting acts, child pornography, and material that advocates or praises acts of terrorism.\\r\\n\\r\\nBy keeping images like this on your electronic devices such as a hard drive, computer or mobile phone, even if someone has sent them to you, you may be committing an offence.\\r\\n\\r\\n##Information Sheet\\r\\nDownload a copy of the Border clearance advice for religious pilgrims information sheet:\\r\\n\\r\\n* [Border clearance advice for religious pilgrims (English) (178KB PDF)](http://www.border.gov.au/News/Documents/religious-pilgrim-factsheet-english.pdf)  \\r\\n* [Border clearance advice for religious pilgrims (Arabic) (239KB PDF)](http://www.border.gov.au/News/Documents/religious-pilgrim-factsheet-arabic.pdf)  \\r\\n* [Border clearance advice for religious pilgrims (Farsi) (234KB PDF)](http://www.border.gov.au/News/Documents/religious-pilgrim-factsheet-farsi.pdf)  \\r\\n* [Border clearance advice for religious pilgrims (Turkish) (210KB PDF)](http://www.border.gov.au/News/Documents/religious-pilgrim-factsheet-turkish.pdf)  ", "short_summary": "We recognise the importance of religious events such as the Hajj, Umrah and Arba’een and the right to travel for such events."}	2016-07-29 00:48:52.393525
275	7	\N	\N	\N	2016-07-22-department-of-finance-supply-nation-s-meet-the-indigenous-supplier-event	0	2016-07-29 00:57:59.77041	2016-07-29 00:58:11.727025	NewsArticle	{"release_date": "2016-07-22"}	published	7aa67710-e35c-4433-8858-0c1ebd63410a	\N	\N	{"name": "Department of Finance / Supply Nation's Meet the Indigenous Supplier event", "options": {}, "content_body": "![The Department of Finance and Supply Nation hosted a 'Meet the Indigenous Supplier' event](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/AusDTO/gov-au-images/master/finance-pres.jpg)\\r\\n\\r\\nNinety-four Indigenous suppliers exhibited and showcased their goods and services to over 350 Commonwealth officers and corporates. Staff from the [Department of the Prime Minister and Cabinet (PM&C)](/department-of-the-prime-minister-and-cabinet), the [Department of Finance](/department-of-finance) and Indigenous Business Australia provided information on the Indigenous Procurement Policy (IPP) and the assistance available to Indigenous businesses.", "short_summary": "The Department of Finance and Supply Nation hosted a 'Meet the Indigenous Supplier' event on 30 June 2016, in Canberra, at the National Convention Centre."}	2016-07-29 00:58:02.580074
72	13	93	\N	About the Minister	about-the-minister	1	2016-07-14 01:34:27.819589	2016-07-29 03:03:01.965458	GeneralContent	\N	published	d5c60b8c-b2fd-43d3-bc13-08278502acab	\N	\N	{"name": "About the Minister", "content_body": "![Hon Peter Dutton MP](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/AusDTO/gov-au-images/master/ministers/peter-dutton-fb.jpg)\\r\\n\\r\\n## The Hon Peter Dutton MP\\r\\n\\r\\nLiberal Party of Australia\\r\\n\\r\\n### Political career\\r\\n\\r\\nMr Dutton was appointed Minister for Immigration and Border Protection on 23 December 2014. He had previously been the Minister for Health and Minister for Sport since the 2013 Federal Election. \\r\\n\\r\\nIn September 2008, he was promoted to Shadow Minister for Health and Ageing.\\r\\n\\r\\nFollowing the change of Government in November 2007, Minister Dutton was promoted to Shadow Cabinet as Minister for Finance, Competition Policy and Deregulation. \\r\\n\\r\\nIn January 2006, Mr Dutton was promoted to Minister for Revenue and Assistant Treasurer. He worked closely with the then Treasurer, Peter Costello, in areas including Budget preparation, taxation and superannuation.\\r\\n\\r\\nIn 2004, Mr Dutton became one of the youngest Ministers since Federation with his appointment as Minister for Workforce Participation, with responsibilities including the Job Network, Disability Employment Services and Work for the Dole.\\r\\n\\r\\nIn November 2001 at the age of 30, he was elected as the Federal Member for Dickson in Queensland.\\r\\n\\r\\n[Read more on his personal website](http://www.peterdutton.com.au/)\\r\\n\\r\\n### Life and career outside politics\\r\\n\\r\\nPrior to entering Parliament, Mr Dutton employed 40 staff in his small business following a successful career as a Queensland police officer, including in the Drug Squad, Sex Offenders Squad and the National Crime Authority.\\r\\n\\r\\nHe is married to Kirilly and is the very proud father of Rebecca, Harry and Tom.​", "short_summary": "The current Minister for Immigration and Border Protection is The Hon Peter Dutton MP, Federal Member for Dickson in Queensland."}	2016-07-29 03:03:01.963089
64	10	90	\N	About the Minister	about-the-minister	1	2016-07-14 01:29:40.970076	2016-07-29 03:03:19.169205	GeneralContent	\N	published	69c5c5ec-0e47-49d3-97ad-63e4f5f5acc5	\N	\N	{"name": "About the Minister", "content_body": "![Senator the Hon Mathias Cormann](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/AusDTO/gov-au-images/master/ministers/mathias-cormann-fb.jpg)\\r\\n\\r\\n## Senator the Hon Mathias Cormann\\r\\n\\r\\nLiberal Party of Australia\\r\\n\\r\\n### Political career\\r\\n\\r\\nSenator the Hon Mathias Cormann was appointed as the [Minister for Finance](/minister-for-finance) on 18 September 2013.\\r\\n\\r\\nHe also serves as the Deputy Leader of the Government in the Senate and Special Minister of State.\\r\\n\\r\\nIn Opposition, Senator Cormann held the positions of Shadow Parliamentary Secretary for Health Administration (2008-09), Shadow Minister for Employment Participation, Apprenticeships and Training (2009-10) and Shadow Assistant Treasurer, Shadow Minister for Financial Services and Superannuation (2010-13).\\r\\n\\r\\nHe also chaired the Senate Select Committees on Fuel and Energy (2008-10) and the Scrutiny of New Taxes (2010-11).\\r\\n\\r\\nSenator Cormann has represented the State of Western Australia in the Senate since June 2007.\\r\\n\\r\\nHe has also worked as a Ministerial Chief of Staff to WA State government Ministers, as Senior Adviser to then Western Australian Premier Richard Court and as an Adviser to then Justice and Customs Minister Chris Ellison.\\r\\n\\r\\nHaving joined the Liberal party in Western Australia in 1996, Mathias was elected Senior Vice President of the Perth Division in 2000, State Vice President in 2003 and State Senior Vice President in 2004, a responsibility he fulfilled until March 2008.\\r\\n\\r\\n### Life and career outside politics\\r\\n\\r\\nMathias was born in Belgium and decided to migrate to Australia permanently after first visiting Perth in 1994, attracted by the great lifestyle and opportunities on offer in Western Australia.\\r\\n\\r\\nBefore migrating to Perth, Mathias had graduated in law at the Flemish university of Louvain (Leuven).\\r\\n\\r\\nBetween 2003 and 2007 Mathias worked for major Western Australian health insurer HBF in a range of senior management roles, including as General Manager HealthGuard and as General Manager of HBF Health Insurance.\\r\\n\\r\\nIn 2001, realising a childhood dream, Mathias obtained his Private Pilots Licence through the Royal Aero Club of Western Australia.\\r\\n\\r\\nMathias is married to Hayley, a lawyer with the Perth office of Clayton Utz and they have two daughters Isabelle and Charlotte.", "short_summary": "The current Minister for Finance is Senator the Hon Mathias Cormann, Senator for Western Australia."}	2016-07-29 03:03:19.166627
63	9	89	\N	About the Minister	about-the-minister	1	2016-07-14 01:04:52.224372	2016-07-29 06:08:31.629933	GeneralContent	\N	published	a43ff8db-6815-4534-9dc7-e224e508ee61	\N	\N	{"name": "About the Minister", "content_body": "![Senator the Hon Mitch Fifield](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/AusDTO/gov-au-images/master/ministers/mitch-fifield-fb.png)\\r\\n\\r\\n## Senator the Hon Mitch Fifield\\r\\n\\r\\nLiberal Party of Australia\\r\\n\\r\\n### Political career\\r\\n\\r\\nSenator Fifield was appointed as the [Minister for Communications](/minister-for-communications), [Minister for the Arts](/minister-for-the-arts) and Minister Assisting the Prime Minister for Digital Government on 21 September 2015.\\r\\n\\r\\nHe also serves as a member of the Prime Minister's Leadership Group and the Senate leadership team, as Manager of Government Business in the Senate.\\r\\n\\r\\nSenator Fifield was previously the Assistant Minister for Social Services, with responsibility for disabilities and ageing.\\r\\n\\r\\nHe was sworn in as Senator for Victoria in the Australian Parliament on 1 April 2004 and re-elected at the 2007 and 2013 elections.\\r\\n\\r\\nBefore entering Parliament, Senator Fifield worked as a senior political adviser to the former Treasurer, Peter Costello, and held senior advisory positions in the Victorian Kennett and New South Wales Greiner governments. \\r\\n\\r\\n[Read more on his personal website](http://www.mitchfifield.com/)\\r\\n\\r\\n### Life and career outside politics\\r\\n\\r\\nSenator Fifield has served as a reservist in the Australian Army Psychology Corps and studied politics at Sydney University.\\r\\n\\r\\nHe was an Ambassador for the not-for-profit school music organisation The Song Room and was an Advisory Board Member for the Yachad Accelerated Learning Project for indigenous students. Mitch was a founding director and is the current chair of the Sir Paul Hasluck Foundation.", "short_summary": "The current Minister for Communications is Senator the Hon Mitch Fifield, Senator for Victoria."}	2016-07-29 06:08:31.627581
91	11	81	\N	\N	prime-minister	9	2016-07-14 04:41:27.116968	2016-08-02 00:44:45.451217	SectionHome	\N	published	dbf6e847-655d-4657-903c-7639677e17aa	\N	\N	{"name": "Prime Minister", "content_body": "## About the Prime Minister\\r\\n\\r\\n![Hon Malcom Turnbull MP](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/AusDTO/gov-au-images/master/ministers/malcom-turnbull-hero.jpg)\\r\\n\\r\\n**The Hon Malcolm Turnbull MP**  \\r\\nLiberal Party of Australia  \\r\\nMr Turnbull was sworn in as the 29th Prime Minister of Australia on 15 September 2015.\\r\\n\\r\\n[More on the Minister](/prime-minister/about-the-prime-minister)\\r\\n\\r\\n## Responsibilities\\r\\n\\r\\nThe Prime Minister is the most powerful person in Parliament. They have many responsibilities including selecting ministers, leading Cabinet in choosing government policies, acting as the chief government spokesperson and representing the Australian Government overseas.\\r\\n\\r\\nThe Prime Minister also works with the [Department of the Prime Minister and Cabinet](department-of-the-prime-minister-and-cabinet) and other government agencies within the Prime Minister and Cabinet portfolio.\\r\\n\\r\\nThe Department and agencies provide information and services related to various portfolio issues such as national security, gender equality, climate change and more.\\r\\n\\r\\n[More about responsibilities](/prime-minister/responsibilities)\\r\\n\\r\\n## Contact\\r\\n\\r\\n**Parliament Office**  \\r\\nPhone number: [02 6277 7700 ](tel:0262777700)\\r\\n\\r\\n[More contact details](/prime-minister/contact)", "short_summary": "The Prime Minister is the leader of the Australian Government and the nation."}	2016-08-02 00:44:45.447775
89	9	81	\N	\N	minister-for-communications	7	2016-07-14 04:41:27.085024	2016-08-02 00:42:20.936361	SectionHome	\N	published	d7d5f6d2-8029-440a-b351-bc8577c503c2	\N	\N	{"name": "Minister for Communications", "content_body": "## About the Minister\\r\\n\\r\\n![Senator the Hon Mitch Fifield](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/AusDTO/gov-au-images/master/ministers/mitch-fifield-hero.jpg)\\r\\n\\r\\n**Senator The Hon Mitch Fifield**  \\r\\nLiberal Party of Australia  \\r\\nSenator Fifield was appointed as the Minister for Communications and Minister for the Arts on 21 September 2015.\\r\\n\\r\\n[More on the Minister](/minister-for-communications/about-the-minister)\\r\\n\\r\\n## Responsibilities\\r\\n\\r\\nThe Minister for Communications leads the [Department of Communications and the Arts](/department-of-communications-and-the-arts) and other agencies within the government's Communications portfolio.\\r\\n\\r\\nThe Department and agencies provide information and services related to the internet, radio, television, phones and more.\\r\\n\\r\\nThe Minister works with government organisations, the community and professional associations to create and update communication laws.\\r\\n\\r\\n[More about responsibilities](/minister-for-communications/responsibilities)\\r\\n\\r\\n## Contact\\r\\n\\r\\n**Parliament Office**  \\r\\nEmail: [minister@communications.gov.au](mailto:minister@communications.gov.au)  \\r\\nPhone number: [02 6277 7480](tel:0262777480)  \\r\\n\\r\\n[More contact details](/minister-for-communications/contact)", "short_summary": "The Minister advises the Australian Government about communication services and technologies."}	2016-08-02 00:42:20.93297
67	10	90	\N	Responsibilities	responsibilities	0	2016-07-14 01:30:50.562246	2016-07-29 04:13:40.502039	GeneralContent	\N	published	0dc2270c-f147-4aa0-98c3-7f8aa3fcef50	\N	\N	{"name": "Responsibilities", "content_body": "The Minister leads the [Department of Finance](/department-of-finance) and other government agencies within the Communications portfolio.\\r\\n\\r\\nMinisters are members of the Australian Government who have been allocated an area of responsibility for how Australia is run. This area of responsibility is known as a portfolio.\\r\\n\\r\\nThe Department and agencies provide information and services related to various portfolio responsibilities such as [the budget, financial reporting, asset management and more](/department-of-finance/our-responsibilities-5d2a387b-45ad-4f19-aab4-819c92cd520b). \\r\\n\\r\\nThe minister works with their department, agencies, community organisations and professional associations to:\\r\\n\\r\\n* Develop policies related to finance issues. A policy states the Government’s plans for an issue and how to achieve them.\\r\\n* Prepare new laws.\\r\\n* Change existing laws which need updating or improving.\\r\\n* Introduce bills (suggestions for new or changed laws) into the Parliament and explain why they are necessary and how they will solve finance problems. If a bill becomes a law, the minister and their department are responsible for putting that law into action.", "short_summary": "The Minister for Finance develops policies and laws to improve the management of Australian Government finances."}	2016-07-29 04:13:40.499689
62	9	89	\N	Responsibilities	responsibilities	0	2016-07-14 01:03:31.319943	2016-08-01 05:50:13.369883	GeneralContent	\N	published	93e6711c-3b5c-4c15-912b-22c0d856c848	\N	\N	{"name": "Responsibilities", "content_body": "The Minister leads the [Department of Communications and the Arts](/department-of-communications-and-the-arts) and other government agencies within the Communications portfolio.\\r\\n\\r\\nMinisters are members of the Australian Government who have been allocated an area of responsibility for how Australia is run. This area of responsibility is known as a portfolio.\\r\\n\\r\\nThe Department and agencies provide information and services related to various portfolio responsibilities such as [television, radio, internet, phone and more.](/department-of-communications-and-the-arts/our-role) \\r\\n\\r\\nThe minister works with their department, agencies, community organisations and professional associations to:\\r\\n\\r\\n* Develop policies related to communication issues. A policy states the Government’s plans for an issue and how to achieve them.\\r\\n* Prepare new laws.\\r\\n* Change existing laws which need updating or improving.\\r\\n* Introduce bills (suggestions for new or changed laws) into the Parliament and explain why they are necessary and how they will solve communication problems. If a bill becomes a law, the minister and their department are responsible for putting that law into action.\\r\\n\\r\\n***\\r\\n\\r\\nInformation sources: Department of Communications website and Parliamentary Education Office website.", "short_summary": "The Minister for Communications develops policies and laws to improve communication services such as television, internet and radio."}	2016-08-01 05:50:13.367547
83	4	81	\N	\N	television-tv-reception	1	2016-07-14 04:41:26.957245	2016-08-01 02:10:20.688756	SectionHome	\N	published	a3a5ee12-52fa-40b5-927c-b5383daadeab	\N	\N	{"name": "Television (TV) reception", "content_body": "###[Setting up digital TV](/television-tv-reception/setting-up-digital-tv)  \\r\\nFind out how to get free-to-air TV using an antenna or a satellite.\\r\\n\\r\\n###[Troubleshooting TV reception](/television-tv-reception/troubleshooting-tv-reception) \\r\\nFollow these steps to fix TV reception and signal issues.  \\r\\n\\r\\n###[Areas with temporary reception issues](/television-tv-reception/areas-with-temporary-reception-issues)  \\r\\nLocations with poor TV reception caused by temporary issues like interference, planned maintenance or storm damage.\\r\\n\\r\\n###[Retransmitting TV channels](/television-tv-reception/retransmitting-tv-channels)   \\r\\nIn remote areas with poor TV reception, local organisations can apply to retransmit channels.  ", "short_summary": "Everyone in Australia should see a clear picture and hear good sound quality with free-to-air digital television (TV). "}	2016-08-01 02:10:20.685186
32	5	84	\N	Contact us	contact-us	8	2016-07-06 00:57:01.480823	2016-07-29 05:54:08.647195	GeneralContent	\N	published	31a59f44-da72-4428-916f-e269dff9487c	\N	\N	{"name": "Contact us", "options": {"toc": 1}, "content_body": "## Email\\r\\n\\r\\n**General enquiries**  \\r\\n[Contact form](http://www.health.gov.au/internet/main/publishing.nsf/Content/health-comments.htm)\\r\\n\\r\\n**Media enquiries**  \\r\\n[news@health.gov.au](mailto:news@health.gov.au)\\r\\n\\r\\n## Phone\\r\\n\\r\\nIf you have a hearing or speech impairment, please use the [National Relay Service](http://relayservice.gov.au/) to access any of our listed phone numbers.  \\r\\nTelephone: [1300 555 727](tel:1300555727)  \\r\\n\\r\\n**General enquiries**  \\r\\n[02 6289 1555](tel:0262891555)  \\r\\nFreecall: [1800 020 103](tel:1800020103)  \\r\\n9am – 5pm AEST (Australian Eastern Standard Time)  \\r\\nMonday to Friday excluding ACT public holidays\\r\\n\\r\\n**Media enquiries**  \\r\\n[02 6289 7400](tel:0262897400)  \\r\\n9am – 5pm AEST (Australian Eastern Standard Time)  \\r\\nMonday to Friday excluding ACT public holidays  \\r\\n\\r\\n## Locations and opening hours\\r\\n\\r\\nSirius Building  \\r\\nFurzer Street  \\r\\nWoden Town Centre ACT.  \\r\\nAccess to the Sirius Building is via the corner of Furzer and Worgan streets.   \\r\\n\\r\\nScarborough House  \\r\\nAtlantic Street  \\r\\nWoden Town Centre ACT.  \\r\\nAccess to Scarborough House is via Atlantic St.  \\r\\n\\r\\n9am – 5pm AEST\\r\\nMonday to Friday excluding ACT public holidays\\r\\n\\r\\n## Postal address\\r\\n\\r\\nDepartment of Health  \\r\\nGPO Box 9848  \\r\\nCanberra ACT 2601  \\r\\nAustralia  \\r\\n\\r\\n## Social media\\r\\n\\r\\n**Facebook​**  \\r\\n[Australian Department of Health](https://www.facebook.com/healthgovau)  \\r\\n\\r\\n**Twitter**  \\r\\n[@healthgovau](https://twitter.com/healthgovau)  \\r\\n\\r\\n**Pinterest**  \\r\\n[Australian Department of Health](https://au.pinterest.com/healthgovau)  \\r\\n\\r\\n**YouTube**  \\r\\n[Department of Health](https://www.youtube.com/user/healthgovau)  \\r\\n\\r\\n**Linkedin**  \\r\\n[Australian Department of Health](https://www.linkedin.com/company/15066)  \\r\\n\\r\\n**Google+**  \\r\\n[Department of Health](https://plus.google.com/115773539901402492627)  \\r\\n\\r\\n## How we handle enquiries\\r\\n\\r\\n**[Client Service Charter](#)**  \\r\\nWe aim to provide you with a high level of service.  \\r\\n\\r\\n**[Complaints Handling Policy](#)**  \\r\\nHow to complain about us and have your concerns addressed.\\r\\n    \\r\\n**[Privacy Policy](#)**  \\r\\nHow we handle personal and sensitive information.  \\r\\n\\r\\n**[Reporting Fraud Policy](#)**  \\r\\nYou are strongly encouraged to report any suspected fraud incidents against us.  \\r\\n    \\r\\n**[Public Interest Disclosure](#)**  \\r\\nHow to report serious wrongdoing in the Commonwealth public sector.  \\r\\n\\r\\n**[Freedom of information (FOI) requests](#)**  \\r\\nLearn more about our FOI obligations and how to make a request.  ", "short_summary": "You can contact us in a number of ways."}	2016-07-29 05:54:08.644517
75	13	93	\N	Responsibilities	responsibilities	0	2016-07-14 01:36:20.234096	2016-07-29 04:12:25.021331	GeneralContent	\N	published	1b3d536c-0fb6-4f1d-acfc-949fee384708	\N	\N	{"name": "Responsibilities", "content_body": "The Minister leads the [Department of Immigration and Border Protection](/department-of-immigration-and-border-protection) and other government agencies within the Immigration and Border Protection portfolio.\\r\\n\\r\\nMinisters are members of the Australian Government who have been allocated an area of responsibility for how Australia is run. This area of responsibility is known as a portfolio.\\r\\n\\r\\nThe Department and agencies provide information and services related to various portfolio responsibilities such as [visas, citizenship and moving to Australia](/department-of-immigration-and-border-protection/our-role).\\r\\n\\r\\nThe minister works with their department, agencies, community organisations and professional associations to:\\r\\n\\r\\n* Develop policies related to immigration and border protection issues. A policy states the Government’s plans for an issue and how to achieve them.\\r\\n* Prepare new laws.\\r\\n* Change existing laws which need updating or improving.\\r\\n* Introduce bills (suggestions for new or changed laws) into the Parliament and explain why they are necessary and how they will solve immigration and border protection problems. If a bill becomes a law, the minister and their department are responsible for putting that law into action.", "short_summary": "The Minister for Immigration and Border Protection develops policies and laws to improve Australia’s immigration and border protection services."}	2016-07-29 04:12:25.019096
90	10	81	\N	\N	minister-for-finance	8	2016-07-14 04:41:27.100335	2016-08-02 00:42:57.34965	SectionHome	\N	published	e1b8c70e-117f-407e-a747-e46bf0596ac7	\N	\N	{"name": "Minister for Finance", "content_body": "## About the Minister\\r\\n\\r\\n![Senator the Hon Mathias Cormann](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/AusDTO/gov-au-images/master/ministers/mathias-cormann-hero.jpg)\\r\\n\\r\\n**Senator the Hon Mathias Cormann**  \\r\\nLiberal Party of Australia  \\r\\nSenator Cormann was appointed as the Minister for Finance on 18 September 2013.  \\r\\n\\r\\n[More on the Minister](/minister-for-finance/about-the-minister)\\r\\n\\r\\n## Responsibilities\\r\\n\\r\\nThe Minister for Finance leads the [Department of Finance](/department-of-finance) and other agencies within the Government's Communications portfolio.\\r\\n\\r\\nThe Department and agencies provide information and services related to the Government’s budget and financial reporting, asset management and more.\\r\\n\\r\\nThe Minister works with government organisations, the community and professional associations to create and update finance laws.\\r\\n\\r\\n[More about responsibilities](/minister-for-finance/responsibilities)\\r\\n\\r\\n## Contact\\r\\n\\r\\n**Parliament Office**  \\r\\nEmail: [financeminister@finance.gov.au](mailto:financeminister@finance.gov.au)  \\r\\nPhone number: [02 6215 2222](tel:0262152222)  \\r\\n\\r\\n[More contact details](/minister-for-finance/contact)", "short_summary": "The Minister advises the Australian Government about financial and business matters such as the budget and reporting processes."}	2016-08-02 00:42:57.346226
92	12	81	\N	\N	minister-for-health-and-aged-care	10	2016-07-14 04:41:27.131234	2016-08-02 00:43:35.28165	SectionHome	\N	published	8c77fdc7-fe0b-4e99-8724-c641c2eed1f2	\N	\N	{"name": "Minister for Health and Aged Care", "content_body": "## About the Minister\\r\\n\\r\\n![The Hon Sussan Ley MP](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/AusDTO/gov-au-images/master/ministers/sussan-ley-hero.jpg)\\r\\n\\r\\n**The Hon Sussan Ley MP**  \\r\\nLiberal Party of Australia  \\r\\nMs Ley was appointed Minister for Health in December 2014 and became Minister for Health and Aged Care on 30 September 2015.\\r\\n\\r\\n[More on the Minister](/minister-for-health-and-aged-care/about-the-minister)\\r\\n\\r\\n## Responsibilities\\r\\n\\r\\nThe Minister for Health and Aged Care leads the [Department of Health](/department-of-health) and other agencies within the government's Health and Aged Care portfolio.\\r\\n\\r\\nThe Department and agencies provide information and services related to health services, diseases, conditions and more.\\r\\n\\r\\nThe Minister works with government organisations, the community and professional associations to create and update health and aged care laws.\\r\\n\\r\\n[More about responsibilities](/minister-for-health-and-aged-care/responsibilities)\\r\\n\\r\\n## Contact\\r\\n\\r\\n**Parliament Office**  \\r\\nEmail: [Minister.Ley@health.gov.au](mailto:Minister.Ley@health.gov.au)  \\r\\nPhone number: [02 6277 7220](tel:0262777220)  \\r\\n\\r\\n[More contact details](/minister-for-health-and-aged-care/contact)", "short_summary": "The Minister advises the Australian Government about Australia’s health and aged care systems."}	2016-08-02 00:43:35.278084
